{"links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/arxiv/1003.3486?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/arxiv/1003.3486?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/arxiv/1003.3486?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/arxiv/1003.3486?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/arxiv/1003.3486?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/arxiv/1003.3486?format=cv"},"id":"1003.3486","metadata":{"publication_info":[{"year":2010,"artid":"084036","journal_title":"Class.Quant.Grav.","journal_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/journals/1214779"},"journal_volume":"27"},{"cnum":"C09-06-21.1","parent_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/858749"},"conference_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/conferences/979813"}}],"citation_count":305,"citation_count_without_self_citations":303,"core":true,"dois":[{"value":"10.1088/0264-9381/27/8/084036"}],"titles":[{"title":"The gravitational-wave memory effect"},{"title":"The gravitational-wave memory effect","source":"arXiv"}],"$schema":"https://inspirehep.net/schemas/records/hep.json","authors":[{"uuid":"d9c90edc-bcf8-4c34-8003-b54ad57acc2b","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1010291"},"full_name":"Favata, Marc","affiliations":[{"value":"Santa Barbara, KITP","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/903889"}}],"signature_block":"FAVATm","curated_relation":true}],"curated":true,"figures":[{"key":"fc8ce5b1a932bba9732c4ca58ac9a5f1","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/fc8ce5b1a932bba9732c4ca58ac9a5f1","source":"arxiv","caption":"Examples of gravitational-wave signals with memory. The left plot shows the waveform modes $\\ddot{I}_{2m}$ for a hyperbolic orbit with eccentricity $e_0=2$ as a function of time [see (\\ref{eq:Ilmhyperbolic}) and surrounding text]. Note the linear memory present in the imaginary part of $\\ddot{I}_{22}$. The right plot shows the $h_{+}$ polarization for an equal-mass binary black hole coalescence with (blue/solid) and without (red/dashed) the nonlinear memory. The oscillatory piece of $h_+$ was computed using an effective-one-body (EOB) model for the $h_{22}$ mode. The memory piece was computed by substituting this mode into (\\ref{eq:memhplus}). See \\cite{favata-memory-saturation} for details.","filename":"f1a_color.png"},{"key":"4de292a10debd967eb9fd447f1f9e9aa","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/4de292a10debd967eb9fd447f1f9e9aa","source":"arxiv","caption":"Examples of gravitational-wave signals with memory. The left plot shows the waveform modes $\\ddot{I}_{2m}$ for a hyperbolic orbit with eccentricity $e_0=2$ as a function of time [see (\\ref{eq:Ilmhyperbolic}) and surrounding text]. Note the linear memory present in the imaginary part of $\\ddot{I}_{22}$. The right plot shows the $h_{+}$ polarization for an equal-mass binary black hole coalescence with (blue/solid) and without (red/dashed) the nonlinear memory. The oscillatory piece of $h_+$ was computed using an effective-one-body (EOB) model for the $h_{22}$ mode. The memory piece was computed by substituting this mode into (\\ref{eq:memhplus}). See \\cite{favata-memory-saturation} for details.","filename":"f1b_color.png"},{"key":"0f66c0f1c92296b613de9986860f2eea","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/0f66c0f1c92296b613de9986860f2eea","source":"arxiv","caption":"Detectability of the memory with LISA. The left plot shows the LISA noise amplitude $h_n$ (solid/black) and the characteristic amplitudes $h_c$ for the inspiral waves (solid/orange) and the nonlinear memory for a black hole binary with the indicated parameters. The short-dashed/blue curve uses the MWM (\\ref{eq:FThmem}) to compute the memory; the long-dashed/red curve uses a truncated-inspiral model based on \\cite{kennefick-memory} (see \\cite{favata-memory-saturation} for details). The right plot shows the angle-averaged SNR of the nonlinear memory signal for equal-mass LISA binaries as a function of the total binary (source-frame) mass $M$ and redshift $z$.","filename":"f2a_color.png"},{"key":"9db5dd486d5a4aaf63be349062df0baf","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/9db5dd486d5a4aaf63be349062df0baf","source":"arxiv","caption":"Detectability of the memory with LISA. The left plot shows the LISA noise amplitude $h_n$ (solid/black) and the characteristic amplitudes $h_c$ for the inspiral waves (solid/orange) and the nonlinear memory for a black hole binary with the indicated parameters. The short-dashed/blue curve uses the MWM (\\ref{eq:FThmem}) to compute the memory; the long-dashed/red curve uses a truncated-inspiral model based on \\cite{kennefick-memory} (see \\cite{favata-memory-saturation} for details). The right plot shows the angle-averaged SNR of the nonlinear memory signal for equal-mass LISA binaries as a function of the total binary (source-frame) mass $M$ and redshift $z$.","filename":"f2b_color.png"}],"texkeys":["Favata:2010zu"],"citeable":true,"keywords":[{"value":"black hole: coalescence","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"black hole: binary","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"gravitational radiation","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"nonlinear","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"interferometer","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"LISA","schema":"INSPIRE"}],"abstracts":[{"value":"The nonlinear memory effect is a slowly-growing, non-oscillatory contribution to the gravitational-wave amplitude. It originates from gravitational waves that are sourced by the previously emitted waves. In an ideal gravitational-wave interferometer a gravitational-wave with memory causes a permanent displacement of the test masses that persists after the wave has passed. Surprisingly, the nonlinear memory affects the signal amplitude starting at leading (Newtonian-quadrupole) order. Despite this fact, the nonlinear memory is not easily extracted from current numerical relativity simulations. After reviewing the linear and nonlinear memory I summarize some recent work, including: (1) computations of the memory contribution to the inspiral waveform amplitude (thus completing the waveform to third post-Newtonian order)/ (2) the first calculations of the nonlinear memory that include all phases of binary black hole coalescence (inspiral, merger, ringdown)/ and (3) realistic estimates of the detectability of the memory with LISA.","source":"arXiv"}],"references":[{"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"65","page_start":"65","journal_title":"Astron.Zh.","journal_volume":"55"}}},{"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"36","page_start":"36","journal_title":"Sov.Astron.Lett.","journal_volume":"22"}}},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/142058"},"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"1100","page_start":"1100","journal_title":"Astrophys.J.","journal_volume":"234"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/838240"},"reference":{"arxiv_eprint":"0911.5206","publication_info":{"artid":"084013","journal_title":"Class.Quant.Grav.","journal_volume":"27"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/830558"},"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"L38","page_start":"L38","journal_title":"Mon.Not.Roy.Astron.Soc.","journal_volume":"400"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/830396"},"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"2372","page_start":"2372","journal_title":"Mon.Not.Roy.Astron.Soc.","journal_volume":"401"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/830305"},"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"417","page_start":"417","journal_title":"Mon.Not.Roy.Astron.Soc.","journal_volume":"402"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/803801"},"reference":{"arxiv_eprint":"0812.0069","publication_info":{"artid":"024002","journal_title":"Phys.Rev.D","journal_volume":"80"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/237866"},"reference"